# Who may sign a response with an ssh key (`war sign --ssh-sign`).
#
# Copy to `allowed_signers` and edit. Like `roles.toml`, this file is written by
# a human in an editor and by nothing else: it is the list of keys whose
# signatures this repository accepts, and a tool that wrote it could let an
# agent add its own key.
#
# Format is OpenSSH's ALLOWED SIGNERS (see ssh-keygen(1), "-Y verify"):
#
#   <principal> [namespaces="oh.war/response"] <keytype> <base64> [comment]
#
# `principal` is the `ssh_principal` on the actor's `roles.toml` entry — a
# separate name because OpenSSH permits no spaces here. The namespaces option
# is optional but recommended: it means a signature this key made for any
# other purpose does not verify as a response.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE THING `war` CANNOT CHECK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Load the key into your agent with confirmation ON:
#
#   ssh-add -c ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
#
# `-c` makes the agent ask YOU, through a dialog, every time the key is used.
# That dialog is the human act; no shell — yours, or an agent's running inside
# your session — can answer it. Without `-c`, any process that can reach the
# agent socket can sign as you, and an AI agent's shell can reach it. `war`
# has no way to tell whether the key was loaded with `-c`. Get this right.
#
# A hardware key (sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com) with touch required is the same
# guarantee, held in silicon.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

brian namespaces="oh.war/response" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA...replace-with-your-key... brian@host
